## 1. Q6 — the multiplicity lift.  **Proved, and it does remove `M`.**

`MultiplicityLift.lean`.  Index the lifted family by
`MultIndex mult = Σ ρ, Fin (mult ρ) × Bool` and put `mult ρ` copies of each of
`a_ρ`, `b_ρ` into the family (`multPairFamily`).  Then

* `card_multIndex` — the lifted family has `2 ∑_ρ m_ρ` members, i.e. multiplicity
  is ordinary cardinality, exactly as in Riemann–von Mangoldt;
* `sum_mult_sq_pairDefect_le_e2` —
  `∑_ρ m_ρ² · Δ_ρ ≤ e₂(Gram(multPairFamily a b mult))`,
  **with no maximum-multiplicity factor**.  The `m_ρ²` copies of the cross pair
  each contribute `Δ_ρ`; same-type duplicate pairs contribute `0`; all other
  pair minors are `≥ 0`.
* `weightedGramSum_le_e2_mult`, `abs_offset_le_of_e2_mult_budget` — the same
  statement in the weighted-sum language of `GramBudgetTransport.lean`, and the
  resulting tube.

This is a clean improvement on the earlier `M · e₂(G)` bound: no separate
multiplicity theorem for `ζ` is needed anywhere downstream.

## 2. Q2/Q9 — anchored-minor reduction.  **Proved, and it is tight.**

`AnchoredMinorConsumer.lean`.  Two layers.

*Family minors.*  `anchorMinorSum v i₀ = ∑_{j ≠ i₀} det Gram(v i₀, v j)` satisfies
`anchorMinorSum_le_e2` (it is at most the full `e₂`, so it is a strictly cheaper
observer: `q − 1` minors instead of `q(q−1)/2`) and
`pairDefect_le_anchorMinorSum` (the target's defect is still dominated by it, at
the anchor `(ρ, false)`).  Coercivity for an arbitrary anchor coordinate is
`gramDefect_ge_anchor_coord_sq_mul`: `b_{i₀} = 0 ⟹ a_{i₀}²‖b‖² ≤ Δ(a,b)`.

*Coordinate minors* (new this cycle).  `coordAnchorMinorSum a b i₀
= ∑_{j ≠ i₀}(a_{i₀}b_j − a_j b_{i₀})²` is the anchored row of the Cauchy–Binet
expansion.  Proved:

* `coordAnchorMinorSum_le_gramDefect` — it is dominated by the full defect, the
  slack being exactly the off-anchor Cauchy–Schwarz defect `AB − C²`;
* `coordAnchorMinorSum_eq_of_anchor` — if `b_{i₀} = 0` it equals `a_{i₀}²‖b‖²`
  **exactly**.

So the answer to Q2 is yes and the reduction is *lossless for the target*.  It
also settles a question the proposal left open: the anchored observable does not
buy a further logarithmic factor on the **zero side**, because it already equals
the target signal there.  Its gain is entirely on the arithmetic side — an
`O(q)`-term observable instead of an `O(q²)`-term one.

## 3. Q3 — local tail transport.  **Proved; the dimensional gain is real.**

`LocalWindow.lean`.  `compress M f = M ∘ (f × f)` for any coordinate selection
`f : κ → n` (no injectivity needed).  Proved: `compress_add` (so
`G_H = A_H + E_H` is literally an identity), `compress_gram` (the compression of
a Gram matrix is the Gram matrix of the restricted family), `compress_comp`
(functoriality of nested windows — the "context restriction" the proposal asked
for), and the quantitative transport

* `abs_trace_compress_le` : `|tr E_H| ≤ q · θ` where `θ` bounds the entries,
* `frobSq_compress_le` : `‖E_H‖_F² ≤ q² θ²`,
* `e2_compress_tail` : `|e₂(A_H) − e₂(G_H)| ≤ …` in terms of `q` and `θ`.

Every constant involves the **window size `q`**, never the ambient `d ≍ LT`.  So
existing global entrywise/operator control does transport to the window with the
improved dimensional factor.  Caveat, stated honestly: the bounds are in terms of
an entrywise bound `θ`; converting an operator-norm bound into an entrywise bound
costs nothing, but converting it into a *Frobenius* bound on the window still
costs `q`.

Also proved here, and used later: `e2_gram_le_half_trace_sq`
(`e₂(G) ≤ ½ (tr G)²` for a Gram matrix) and the count-and-norm budget
`e₂_multFamily_le_of_norm_bound` : if every vector has `‖v‖² ≤ B` and the lifted
family has `N` members then `e₂ ≤ ½ N² B²`, with the tube corollary
`local_tube_of_norm_bound`.

## 4. Hot/cold and Schur (the architectural requests)

`LocalHotCold.lean` implements the "retain the omitted world as residual"
requirement as literal theorems rather than record fields:
`reopen_hotBlock_coldResidual` (exact reopening `reopen (hot M) (cold M) = M`),
`hotBlock_reopen`, `coldResidual_reopen`, `hotBlock_surjective`, and the
universal property `consumer_factors_through_hotBlock` — a consumer that cannot
distinguish matrices with equal window blocks factors **uniquely** through the
hot state.  `windowAnchorSum` (the anchored observable of the window block) is
then shown hot-determined and to agree with the geometric anchored sum on Gram
matrices (`windowAnchorSum_gram`).

`LocalSchur.lean` proves the real Schur statements the abstract sockets in
`BlockSchurStructure.agda` / `BlockSchurCoercivity.agda` only *assume*
(`subtractLowerBound`, `highBlockCoercive`, `crossCorrectionBounded`,
`schurQuadraticIdentity` are record fields there, i.e. hypotheses, not theorems).
Proved here for real matrices: the variational identity `schur_form_le_full_form`
and `schur_form_eq_at_optimum`, the coercivity transfer `schur_coercive`
(`D ≥ δ`, `CA⁻¹B ≤ η` ⟹ `S ≥ δ − η`), and — the decisive one for our purpose —

> `schur_form_le_compression_form` : the Schur form is **at most** the plain
> compression form.

Consequence: replacing the principal compression `P_H G P_H*` by the effective
Schur object `S_H = G_H − C G_out⁻¹ B` can only *lower* the local quadratic form.
Since the localization programme needs a **lower** bound on the local observable,
the Schur route cannot improve on plain compression here.  That is a negative
answer to the "Schur is a better localization than truncation" suggestion, in
the direction we need; Schur remains the right object if one ever needs an upper
bound or an exact reopening.

## 5. Q1 — local retention at bounded height.  **Quantitative, not free.**

`LocalHeight.lean`, on top of the centred-grid decomposition
`B_full = 2(B_fin + B_tail)` of `RiemannCenteredGridRetentionExact`.

* `tail_le_of_window_length` — a window retaining `m` positive coordinates has
  lattice tail at most half the floor as soon as
  `256 · derivMass(φ,α)² ≤ 9π² m L² α²`;
* `localGramDefect_floor_of_window_length` — under that condition the window
  already carries the full floor `(81/16384) L⁶ α² ≤ Δ_H`;
* `tail_le_of_window_height` — in terms of the ordinate height `H = m h`,
  `h = 2π/L`, the condition reads `512 · derivMass(φ,α)² ≤ 9π H L³ α²`;
* `unit_height_dichotomy` — hence for `H = 1`: **either**
  `|α| ≤ √(512 derivMass²/(9π L³)) ≍ L^{−3/2}`, **or** the unit-height window
  carries the whole floor.

So the answer to "is `H = 1` enough?" is: yes, precisely when
`|α| ≳ derivMass · L^{−3/2}`; for smaller offsets the required height grows like
`derivMass²/(L³α²)`.  This is the honest form of the proposal's hope that a fixed
height suffices — a fixed height suffices *outside a shrinking neighbourhood of
the line*, which is exactly the regime the argument is trying to rule out.  The
"`O(log T)` Fourier samples in a unit window" intuition is correct as far as it
goes; what it does not control is the `α`-dependence of the tail.

## 6. Q7/Q8 — the local trace scale.  **The predicted `L` is false; the truth is `L²`.**

`LocalTraceScale.lean`.  The proposal's step 2 assumed a per-vector bound
`tr G_W^mult ≲ C_vec · L · N_mult(W)`, i.e. `‖v‖² ≲ L` for each grid vector.
That is false for the source vectors.  Proved:

* `sum_normSq_gridVec_le` — a **window-length-free** upper bound
  `∑_k ‖v_k‖² ≤ taperMass² + 2 derivMass²/h²`, and in the source normalisation
  `h = 2π/L`, `gridVec_normSq_source_le` :
  `‖v‖² ≤ taperMass(φ,α)² + derivMass(φ,α)² L²/(2π²)`;
* `gridVec_re_normSq_ge_plateau` — a matching lower bound from the plateau:
  `‖Re v‖² ≥ (2a)² ≥ 9L²/16`;
* `not_linear_trace_bound` — consequently **no** bound of the form `‖v‖² ≤ C_vec·L`
  can hold once `16 C_vec < 9 L`.

The true scale is `‖v‖² ≍ L²`.  This is not a technicality: it is the whole
difference between the proposal's predicted `(log T)⁴` local budget and what the
route actually gives.

## 7. Q5/Q6 (scale) — what the localized budget really is.  **`L⁶`: critical, not decaying.**

`LocalBudgetScale.lean` and `LocalRouteCapstone.lean`.

Feeding the count-and-norm budget `e₂ ≤ ½ N² B²` (`localNormCountBudget N B
= 2N²B²` for the lifted family, which has `N ≍ 2 ∑ m_ρ`) with

* `N ≍ A · L` (bounded-height Riemann–von Mangoldt, counting multiplicity), and
* `B ≍ C_b · L²` (the **proved** scale of §6, not the assumed `C_vec · L`),

gives budget `≍ 2A²C_b² L⁶`, exactly the order of the floor `c L⁶ α²`.  Proved:

* `linear_scale_ratio_tendsto_zero` — *if* `B ≍ L` had been true, the ratio
  `budget / L⁶` would indeed tend to `0` (the proposal's `(log T)^{−2}`);
* `quadratic_scale_ratio_eq_const` — with the true `B ≍ C_b L²`, the ratio is the
  **constant** `2A²C_b²`;
* `quadratic_scale_not_tendsto_zero` — hence it does not tend to `0`;
* `quadratic_scale_tube_radius_const` — the resulting tube radius is a constant.

`LocalRouteCapstone.lean` states the same conclusion in the taper variables,
with no arithmetic input whatsoever:

* `finiteGridGramDefect_le_normBound_sq` — Δ ≤ `Bφ(α,L)²` where
  `Bφ(α,L) = taperMass² + derivMass² L²/(2π²)`, by Cauchy–Schwarz plus §6;
* `source_local_route_constraint` — combining with the §5 floor,
  `(81/16384) L⁶ α² ≤ Bφ(α,L)²`;
* `offset_le_of_quadratic_norm_bound` — and *if* one had an `α`-uniform
  `Bφ ≤ C_b L²`, then `|α| ≤ (128/9) C_b / L`.

The last item isolates precisely what would have to be earned.  It is not
available for free: `taperMass(φ,α) = ∫|φ| e^{−αu}` and
`derivMass(φ,α) = ∫|φ'| e^{−αu}` are taken against a taper of width `≍ L`, so in
the off-line direction they grow like `e^{αL/2}`.  (That growth is asserted here
as prose, not proved in Lean; what *is* proved is the conditional statement
above, which is the form the programme needs.)

So the summary answers:

| question | answer |
|---|---|
| Q1 local retention at fixed `H` | **yes, conditionally** — `H = 1` works iff `\|α\| ≳ derivMass·L^{−3/2}` (`unit_height_dichotomy`) |
| Q2 anchored-minor reduction | **yes**, and lossless at the anchor (`coordAnchorMinorSum_eq_of_anchor`) |
| Q3 local tail transport | **yes** — constants involve `q ≍ LH`, not `d ≍ LT` |
| Q4 prime-source audit below `thm_traces` | **partially** — see §8 |
| Q5 local prime scale `E_prime,loc(T,1) = o(L⁶)` | **not available** — genuinely new short-interval analytic number theory |
| Q6 literal target contradiction | **no** — the localized budget is `≍ L⁶`, critical; the tube radius is constant |

## 8. Q4 — audit of the companion below `PrimeSide.thm_traces`

Companion `Zeta23`, as vendored under `Zeta23Bridge/.lake/packages/Zeta23`.

*Uniform-in-`k` material does exist.*  `GentryA k l = ∫ φ̂(τ−τ_k) φ̂(τ−τ_l) ν_X(τ) dτ`
is defined **entrywise**; `GentryA_diag_eq` is an entrywise diagonal identity;
and the archimedean μ-part carries a per-`k` uniform bound
`|Gμ_k − 2π a L_μ(τ_k)| ≤ (K I₁ + 10 I₂)/T` (`Zeta23/PrimeSideA.lean`, ≈ lines
115–140).  So the archimedean side localizes for free.

*The prime part does not.*  The prime contributions enter only through
`Mform Φ T u₁ u₂ = ∬_{[T,2T]²} Φ(τ−τ')² u₁ u₂` and through `prop_PP`
(`Zeta23/PrimeSideB/PP.lean`, `PPOffDiag.lean`), whose inputs are Hilbert-type
Montgomery–Vaughan (`MVHilbert`) and Chebyshev–Mertens over the **whole dyadic
interval** (`Zeta23/Hypotheses.lean`, `PaperInputs`).  These are mean-value
estimates: they are only good after averaging over `[T, 2T]`.

Conclusion: the fork identified in the proposal resolves to the **harder case**.
A bounded-height version of the prime-side second moment is a short-interval
Dirichlet-polynomial mean-value theorem and is not derivable from the present
inputs.  That said, §7 shows this is no longer the *binding* obstruction: even a
perfect local prime bound would face a floor and a self-budget of the same
order `L⁶`.

## 9. The `o(1)`-versus-`= ½` distinction, formalized

The proposal correctly warned against conflating `β_ρ − ½ → 0` with `β_ρ = ½`.
This is now a pair of theorems rather than a caution:

* `fixed_offset_eq_zero_of_eventual_tube` — for a **fixed** offset `α`, if `α` is
  eventually inside a tube of radius `r(T) → 0`, then `α = 0`;
* `exists_nonzero_offsets_within_shrinking_tube` — but there exists a family of
  **nonzero** offsets `α_n` with `|α_n| ≤ r(n) → 0`.

So a family of bounded-height localization statements, one per window, gives at
best `β − ½ = o(1)` as the height grows, and never `β = ½` — unless the same zero
can be placed in windows of unboundedly large scale parameter, which its ordinate
forbids.  Any future closure of this programme must supply that step explicitly.

